                <section class="content-section" id="best-practices">
                    <h2>Best Practices</h2>
                    <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                        <div class="info-card-title">1. Stay Close to OOTB</div>
                        <p>
                            ServiceNow strongly recommends staying as close to out-of-the-box configurations 
                            as possible. Custom table structures, modified state models, and overridden business 
                            rules create technical debt and complicate upgrades.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                        <div class="info-card-title">2. Invest in CMDB Quality</div>
                        <p>
                            SecOps is only as good as your CMDB. If CI records are incomplete, outdated, or 
                            have no service mapping, SecOps can't provide business context. Run CMDB health 
                            checks regularly and enforce data governance.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                        <div class="info-card-title">3. Start with Governance, Not Automation</div>
                        <p>
                            Before automating heavily, establish governance — define severity criteria, 
                            response SLAs, escalation paths, and role assignments. Automating a bad process 
                            just makes it fail faster.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                        <div class="info-card-title">4. Use Store Apps for Integrations</div>
                        <p>
                            Check the ServiceNow Store before building custom integrations. Pre-built apps for 
                            Splunk, CrowdStrike, Qualys, Tenable, etc. are tested, supported, and upgraded by 
                            ServiceNow. Custom integrations should be a last resort.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                        <div class="info-card-title">5. Implement Alert Deduplication Early</div>
                        <p>
                            One of the biggest operational challenges is alert fatigue from duplicate incidents. 
                            Implement deduplication logic (matching on source, target, rule, and time window) 
                            before connecting your SIEM, or you'll drown in noise.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="content-section" id="pitfalls">
                    <h2>Common Pitfalls</h2>
                    <div class="info-card info-card--warning">
                        <div class="info-card-title">⚠ Ignoring CI Matching in VR</div>
                        <p>
                            If your scanner reports assets by IP but your CMDB uses hostnames, VIs will be 
                            created with temporary CIs. This breaks prioritization, assignment, and reporting. 
                            Always validate CI matching rules before going live with scanner integrations.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card info-card--warning">
                        <div class="info-card-title">⚠ Over-Enriching Observables</div>
                        <p>
                            Querying every observable against every enrichment source wastes API calls and 
                            money. Filter out private IPs, internal domains, and known-safe hashes before 
                            sending to external services. Set up allowlists for your own infrastructure.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card info-card--warning">
                        <div class="info-card-title">⚠ Not Testing Playbooks End-to-End</div>
                        <p>
                            Playbooks that work perfectly in a demo environment can fail in production due to 
                            permission issues, API rate limits, or unexpected data formats. Always test playbooks 
                            end-to-end with realistic data in a sub-production instance.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card info-card--warning">
                        <div class="info-card-title">⚠ Modifying Process Definitions</div>
                        <p>
                            Changing the built-in NIST/SANS process definitions can break playbook execution 
                            and reporting. If you need a custom state model, create a new process definition 
                            rather than modifying the existing ones.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

